#!/bin/bash
# cc-bats — the QoS CHOKEPOINT for the bats gate corpus.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (row 13, MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2.md §4 M1). A Darwin QoS band is the repo's chosen
# answer to gate-vs-interactive contention (row 1 deleted load-gated admission control because
# "a shedder that WAITS amplifies" — R1). But that band was applied INSIDE ONE
# CALLER, scripts/postland-verify.sh, and a census of the live box on 2026-07-29 measured what
# that actually covers:
#
#     72 of 103 live bats procs at pri=31 (full interactive priority)
#     coverage: 30% of procs, 0% of CPU
#
# The bypassing invocation is the ORDINARY one. A live session was running
# `timeout 5400 bats tests/postland-verify.bats` at nice 0 — exactly what CLAUDE.md instructs a
# session to do before committing. `which bats` resolves the real Homebrew binary; there was no
# wrapper, hence no chokepoint.
#
# THE INVERSION: stop asking the caller to demote itself (measured to fail 70% of the time) and
# make the TOOL demote itself. Installed as `bats` earlier on PATH, every invocation — hand-typed
# by an agent, called from a script, fired from launchd — lands in the UTILITY band and yields
# to interactive sessions. Nobody can forget, because there is nothing to remember.
#
# THE BAND IS `utility`, NOT `background` (M1-rev, 2026-07-30; §11.9 per lead message). This file
# shipped with `nice -n 19` + `-c background`. Both halves of that prefix were then MEASURED, and
# both were wrong:
#   · `-c background` is a ~84–89× tax on a long batch job. It confines the process to the 2 E-cores
#     — shared with ~628 system processes — at I/O tier 2, with a ×84 fork penalty on top. A gate
#     run demoted that hard does not yield politely, it fails to finish. `-c utility` costs ~2.4×
#     under the same load (PRI 20, P-core-eligible, tier-1 I/O) and STILL yields to interactive
#     work at PRI 31, which is the only property this file ever needed.
#   · `nice -n 19` NEVER DEMOTED ANYTHING. It moves NI to 19 and leaves PRI at 31 (re-verified on
#     this box 2026-07-30). It was in the prefix on the assumption that it composed with the clamp;
#     it does not. So it is GONE, along with the CC_BATS_NICE/CC_BATS_NICE_BIN seams and the
#     nice-only PARTIAL tier that existed only to fall back to it.
# Band calibration on this box, for the record: utility → PRI 20 (pinned, no drift) · background →
# PRI 4 · maintenance → PRI 4 · nice-19-alone → PRI 31 · plain → PRI 31. The pre-M1-rev background
# calibration stays in the plan as history; this file states what it does NOW.
#
# NOT admission control. Nothing here waits, sleeps, queues, or polls load. Demotion only (R1).
#
# Seams — ALL this file actually reads, each with its empty-value discipline stated. Two kinds:
#   VALUE seams hand the script something it then USES (a path, a band). These take `${VAR+set}`,
#     never `${VAR:-}`, so set-but-EMPTY is honoured VERBATIM — a seam that cannot turn a thing OFF
#     is not a seam.
#   TOGGLE seams are only ever compared against a literal ("off", "1"). Empty is simply not that
#     literal, so `${VAR:-<default>}` and `${VAR+set}` are behaviourally IDENTICAL here; `:-` is
#     used and carries no discipline claim either way.
#
#   CC_BATS_QOS=off          → [toggle] exec the real bats verbatim, no demotion at all (kill
#                              switch, R8). Empty ≡ unset ≡ on.
#   CC_BATS_QUIET=1          → [toggle] suppress the degradation notice on stderr (CI noise control
#                              only; can NEVER suppress the QoS-not-applied notice).
#   CC_BATS_REAL=<path>      → [value] pin the real bats (tests use this; never guesses in
#                              production). SET-BUT-EMPTY honoured verbatim ⇒ resolves to nothing ⇒
#                              FATAL 127, which is how a test asserts the unresolvable-bats path.
#   CC_BATS_SEED=<path>      → [value] pin the activation-recorded seed FILE. SET-BUT-EMPTY honoured
#                              verbatim ⇒ the seed lookup is disabled outright.
#   CC_BATS_TASKPOLICY=<p>   → [value] pin the taskpolicy(8) binary; SET-BUT-EMPTY disables the
#                              clamp ⇒ mode NONE (bats runs UNDEMOTED) WITH the loud notice below.
#   CC_BATS_BAND=<clamp>     → [value] pin the QoS clamp (utility|background|maintenance), default
#                              utility (M1-rev: background is a measured ~84-89x tax on long batch —
#                              MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2.md §11.9). SET-BUT-EMPTY ⇒ mode NONE, loudly.
#
# NOT a seam — an INTERNAL flag, do not set it by hand:
#   CC_BATS_ACTIVE=1         → the re-entrancy marker this script exports for its OWN nested
#                              re-execs (bats re-execs itself; see the guard below). Set externally,
#                              it makes the shim hand straight off to the real bats and apply NO
#                              demotion — the exact silent-inert failure this file exists to
#                              prevent. It is why tests/qos-chokepoint.bats unsets the whole
#                              CC_BATS_* family before each test rather than only the seams it sets.
#
# FAIL LOUD WHEN INERT (R4): TWO modes now, not three. Either the clamp is applied (FULL) or it is
# not (NONE) — and NONE always says so on stderr, even under CC_BATS_QUIET. There is no middle tier,
# because the thing the middle tier fell back to (nice alone) was measured to demote nothing.
# A QoS shim that silently no-ops is a non-feature — ~100% abstain means inert by construction, and
# the whole defect this file fixes was a mechanism that looked applied and was not.
#
# bash 3.2 SAFE: no `case` inside command substitution, no IFS-on-control-char splitting
# (memory bash32-case-in-substitution-zsh-repro-trap). Ships to launchd ⇒ tested under /bin/bash.

set -uo pipefail

# Shim-version stamp. NO CONSUMER TODAY — said plainly (an unbuilt mechanism described as built is
# the same defect class as a feature that abstains 100% of the time, R4). The census classifies
# purely by pri, and that is the RIGHT design: pri is kernel ground truth, whereas this variable is
# a self-report by the very process being judged — a shim that failed to demote would still export
# it. KEPT anyway: it costs one env slot, survives into a crash record / `ps eww` / `sample`, and is
# the correct channel IF an attribution consumer is ever wanted.
# BUMPED to 2 by M1-rev: version 1 emitted nice+background (PRI 4), version 2 emits a clamp
# (default utility, PRI 20). Without the bump a record could not tell a v1 proc that SHOULD read 4
# from a v2 proc that SHOULD read 20, and the two are now different verdicts.
export CC_BATS_VERSION=2

# ── resolve the REAL bats, never ourselves ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Self-shadowing is the whole point of this file (we are installed AS `bats`), so naive
# `command -v bats` would resolve to this script and fork-bomb. Resolve by walking PATH and
# skipping any candidate whose physical path equals ours.
# phys_of <path> → the FULLY symlink-resolved absolute path, or empty on failure.
#
# WHY NOT `cd $(dirname X) && pwd -P` (what this file shipped with, and the bug review killed):
# `pwd -P` physicalises the DIRECTORY only and never the final symlink, so the result is still the
# SYMLINK's path. Two different symlink paths to the SAME script therefore compare as DIFFERENT
# files, the self-skip below cannot fire, and with two shims on PATH you get a closed 2-cycle:
# shim A execs shim B, B (already CC_BATS_ACTIVE=1) execs A, forever. Measured in review: rc=137,
# ~200 fork+exec/s, ZERO output, sustained — 134 resolution legs split exactly 67/67 between the two
# paths. That is the whole reason "shadow mode" was rejected, and it is a latent defect here even
# with one shim installed.
#
# /usr/bin/readlink is addressed ABSOLUTELY on purpose: this function must not depend on PATH (a shim
# installed as `bats` gets invoked from arbitrary PATHs, and an empty command substitution here is
# what turns the guard into the loop). The dir-physical form is kept only as a LAST-RESORT fallback,
# and callers treat an empty return as "cannot establish identity ⇒ refuse", never as "not equal".
phys_of() {
  local p="${1:-}" out d b
  [ -n "$p" ] || return 1
  if [ -x /usr/bin/readlink ]; then
    out=$(/usr/bin/readlink -f -- "$p" 2>/dev/null) || out=""
    [ -n "$out" ] && { printf '%s' "$out"; return 0; }
  fi
  # fallback: dir-physical + basename (weaker — cannot see a sibling symlink to the same file)
  d="${p%/*}"; [ "$d" = "$p" ] && d="."
  b="${p##*/}"
  d=$(cd -- "$d" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P) || return 1
  [ -n "$d" ] && [ -n "$b" ] || return 1
  printf '%s/%s' "$d" "$b"
}

# BUILTINS ONLY — this block must not depend on PATH, and must never guess.
#
# The first version used `$(dirname …)` / `$(basename …)`, which was an UNBOUNDED EXEC LOOP waiting
# to happen (found in review 2026-07-29). If dirname/basename are not resolvable on PATH — which is
# exactly the situation a shim installed as `bats` can be invoked from — the command substitutions
# yield empty, and `cd ""` SILENTLY SUCCEEDS. Both $self_phys and every $cand_phys then collapse to
# cwd-derived garbage differing only by the trailing `/bats`, so no candidate ever compares equal to
# self, the shim execs ITSELF, and it spins a core forever. Worse, a valid seed does NOT rescue it:
# $seeded_phys collapses to the same garbage and is rejected as "self". The guard inverted in both
# directions at once.
#
# So: parameter expansion (no forks, no PATH), and if we still cannot establish our own identity we
# REFUSE (127) rather than proceed blind. An exec loop on a machine-wide `bats` is far worse than a
# loud failure — and "cannot determine self" is a THIRD state, not a reason to continue.
self_phys=""
if [ -n "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-}" ]; then
  self_phys="$(phys_of "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" || self_phys=""
fi
if [ -z "$self_phys" ]; then
  echo "cc-bats: FATAL — cannot determine my own path (BASH_SOURCE='${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-}')." >&2
  echo "cc-bats: refusing to run: without a self-identity the self-skip guard inverts into an exec loop." >&2
  exit 127
fi

# Seed file: the ABSOLUTE path of the real bats, recorded from DISK TRUTH at activation time.
# It exists only when the operator has activated the deeper "shadow the Homebrew path" mode
# (activation 22 — shadow mode). Why a seed rather than a Cellar glob: the glob is version-shaped
# (/opt/homebrew/Cellar/bats-core/<ver>/bin/bats) and picking a version by sort order is a GUESS —
# on a machine with two Cellar versions installed it can silently select the wrong one. The
# activation reads the symlink's CURRENT target before replacing it and writes that exact path here,
# so the shim never has to infer which binary Homebrew considered canonical.
# The seed records a MACHINE fact (where Homebrew's real bats lives), NOT an account fact, so it is
# deliberately keyed on $HOME/.claude and NOT on $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. Measured 2026-07-29: this box runs
# sessions across four config dirs (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR was ~/.claude-secondary in the session that wrote
# this), so a per-config-dir seed would be written once by an activation and then be INVISIBLE to the
# other three accounts' sessions — they would silently fall through to the version-guessing Cellar
# sweep. One machine, one seed.
# Seam honoured VERBATIM including set-but-EMPTY (`+set`, never `:-`): `CC_BATS_SEED=` genuinely
# disables the seed lookup. The `:-` form here was a real asymmetry against this file's own stated
# discipline and against scripts/bats-shim-parity-lint.sh — caught in review.
if [ -n "${CC_BATS_SEED+set}" ]; then
  CC_BATS_SEED_PATH="$CC_BATS_SEED"
else
  CC_BATS_SEED_PATH="$HOME/.claude/state/cc-bats-real"
fi

resolve_real_bats() {
  # 1 — explicit pin (tests, and any caller that knows better). Honoured even if non-executable
  #     so a test can assert the failure path.
  if [ -n "${CC_BATS_REAL+set}" ]; then printf '%s' "$CC_BATS_REAL"; return 0; fi
  # 1.5 — the activation-recorded seed. Tried BEFORE the PATH walk because in shadow mode the
  #       Homebrew path on PATH *is* this shim, and the seed is the only non-guessing answer.
  #       A seed pointing at something absent/non-executable is IGNORED rather than fatal — a stale
  #       seed (brew upgraded the Cellar out from under it) must degrade to the search below, not
  #       break every gate run on the machine.
  if [ -n "$CC_BATS_SEED_PATH" ] && [ -f "$CC_BATS_SEED_PATH" ]; then
    local seeded seeded_phys
    seeded=$(head -1 "$CC_BATS_SEED_PATH" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]')
    if [ -n "$seeded" ] && [ -x "$seeded" ] && [ ! -d "$seeded" ]; then
      seeded_phys="$(phys_of "$seeded")" || seeded_phys=""
      # never accept a seed that resolves back to us — that is the fork-bomb
      if [ -n "$seeded_phys" ] && [ "$seeded_phys" != "$self_phys" ]; then printf '%s' "$seeded"; return 0; fi
    fi
  fi
  # 2 — walk PATH, skipping ourselves by PHYSICAL path (a symlinked shim dir must not fool us).
  local oldifs="$IFS" d cand cand_phys
  IFS=:
  for d in $PATH; do
    IFS="$oldifs"
    [ -n "$d" ] || continue
    cand="$d/bats"
    if [ -x "$cand" ] && [ ! -d "$cand" ]; then
      cand_phys="$(phys_of "$cand")" || cand_phys=""
      # empty cand_phys = could not resolve ⇒ SKIP it. Treating unresolvable as
      # "different from me" is exactly how the guard inverted into an exec loop.
      if [ -n "$cand_phys" ] && [ "$cand_phys" != "$self_phys" ]; then printf '%s' "$cand"; IFS="$oldifs"; return 0; fi
    fi
    IFS=:
  done
  IFS="$oldifs"
  # 3 — known install locations, last resort. Order: Homebrew arm64, Homebrew x86, MacPorts.
  for cand in /opt/homebrew/bin/bats /usr/local/bin/bats /opt/local/bin/bats; do
    if [ -x "$cand" ]; then
      cand_phys="$(phys_of "$cand")" || cand_phys=""
      [ -n "$cand_phys" ] && [ "$cand_phys" != "$self_phys" ] && { printf '%s' "$cand"; return 0; }
    fi
  done
  # 4 — Cellar/opt sweep, reached only in shadow mode with a stale-or-absent seed (every path above
  #     resolved to US). Ordered newest-version-last by `sort -V` so the tail is the highest
  #     version. This IS a guess, which is exactly why the seed exists and is tried first — but a
  #     guess that runs the right tests beats exiting 127 and failing every gate on the box.
  local c found=""
  for c in $(find /opt/homebrew/Cellar/bats-core /usr/local/Cellar/bats-core \
                  /opt/homebrew/opt/bats-core -maxdepth 3 -name bats -type f \
                  -perm -u+x 2>/dev/null | sort -V); do
    [ -x "$c" ] || continue
    cand_phys="$(phys_of "$c")" || cand_phys=""
    [ -n "$cand_phys" ] && [ "$cand_phys" != "$self_phys" ] && found="$c"
  done
  [ -n "${found:-}" ] && { printf '%s' "$found"; return 0; }
  return 1
}

REAL_BATS=$(resolve_real_bats) || REAL_BATS=""
if [ -z "$REAL_BATS" ] || [ ! -x "$REAL_BATS" ]; then
  echo "cc-bats: FATAL — cannot resolve the real bats(1) (self=${self_phys:-?}). Set CC_BATS_REAL." >&2
  exit 127
fi

# ── kill switch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if [ "${CC_BATS_QOS:-on}" = "off" ]; then
  exec "$REAL_BATS" "$@"
fi

# ── re-entrancy guard ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bats re-execs itself (observed: two `bats-core/bats` procs per run). The demotion is idempotent
# — nice 19 twice is still 19, taskpolicy -c background twice is a no-op — so a second wrap would
# be HARMLESS but not FREE: it costs an extra fork+exec pair per nested call, and this file exists
# to reduce load, not add it. Once we are inside the band, hand straight off to the real binary.
if [ "${CC_BATS_ACTIVE:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  exec "$REAL_BATS" "$@"
fi
export CC_BATS_ACTIVE=1

# ── resolve taskpolicy(8) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hooks and launchd jobs run WITHOUT Homebrew on PATH, so absolute paths are tried as well as PATH
# (the same lesson lead-crash-watchdog.sh:23 records for timeout(1)).
if [ -n "${CC_BATS_TASKPOLICY+set}" ]; then
  TP="$CC_BATS_TASKPOLICY"           # set-but-empty honoured verbatim ⇒ QOS_MODE=NONE
else
  TP=""
  for c in /usr/sbin/taskpolicy "$(command -v taskpolicy 2>/dev/null || true)"; do
    if [ -n "$c" ] && [ -x "$c" ]; then TP="$c"; break; fi
  done
fi

# ── resolve the band, and REFUSE an unknown clamp ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-07-30: taskpolicy(8) parses ONLY utility|background|maintenance and on anything else
# exits 64 WITHOUT RUNNING THE PROGRAM. Since this file EXECs taskpolicy with bats as its argument, an
# unvalidated band would not merely fail to demote — it would stop the gate from running at all, and
# the caller would see a bare rc 64 with no bats output. Validate, then degrade to NONE-with-warning.
BAND="${CC_BATS_BAND:-utility}"
BAND_OK=0
case "$BAND" in
  utility|background|maintenance) BAND_OK=1 ;;
esac

# ── decide the mode, and FAIL LOUD about anything we could not get (R4) ────────────────────────
# TWO outcomes now, both explicit — never a silent pass:
#   FULL   taskpolicy -c <band>   (scheduler AND I/O tier clamped)
#   NONE   no clamp applied       (notice on stderr ALWAYS, even under CC_BATS_QUIET —
#                                  "QoS requested but not applied" must never be quiet)
# The old PARTIAL (nice-only) tier is DELETED: it fell back to something measured to demote nothing
# (the full 2x2 calibration lives in scripts/qos-census.sh's header and MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2 §11.9 —
# nice(1) moves NI and nothing else; taskpolicy(8) moves PRI and the I/O tier and nothing else).
# NONE stays REACHABLE and therefore PROVABLE through CC_BATS_TASKPOLICY= alone, which is why
# deleting the CC_BATS_NICE_BIN seam costs no test coverage — that seam existed only to make this
# branch reachable, and the taskpolicy seam already does that.
QOS_MODE="NONE"
if [ -n "$TP" ] && [ -x "$TP" ] && [ "$BAND_OK" = "1" ]; then
  QOS_MODE="FULL"
fi

if [ "$QOS_MODE" = "NONE" ]; then
  # Unconditional — CC_BATS_QUIET may never reach this line. This is the inert case the census must
  # never read as success. Calibration behind the claim (this box, 2026-07-30):
  #   taskpolicy -c utility     -> PRI 20   (P-core-eligible, tier-1 I/O — the band we want)
  #   taskpolicy -c background  -> PRI 4    (E-core confined; ~84-89x tax on long batch)
  #   nice -n 19 alone          -> PRI 31   (NOT demoted — why there is no nice fallback)
  #   plain                     -> PRI 31
  if [ "$BAND_OK" != "1" ]; then
    echo "cc-bats: WARNING — CC_BATS_BAND='$BAND' is not a taskpolicy(8) QoS clamp (want utility|background|maintenance); running bats at FULL interactive priority (PRI 31). QoS NOT applied." >&2
  else
    echo "cc-bats: WARNING — taskpolicy(8) unavailable, and nice(1) alone does NOT move PRI off 31 on Darwin, so there is no fallback that demotes; running bats at FULL interactive priority. QoS NOT applied." >&2
  fi
fi

# The applied-mode half of the CC_BATS_VERSION stamp above, and it carries the SAME status: no
# consumer reads it today — it is an after-the-fact audit aid (a crash record or a live `ps eww`
# can show a given run was FULL or NONE), never an input to any verdict. The census decides by pri,
# which is kernel ground truth rather than this process's own account of itself. The BAND goes with
# it because PRI alone cannot tell which clamp was REQUESTED, only which one landed.
export CC_BATS_QOS_MODE="$QOS_MODE"
export CC_BATS_QOS_BAND="$BAND"

if [ "$QOS_MODE" = "FULL" ]; then
  exec "$TP" -c "$BAND" "$REAL_BATS" "$@"
else
  exec "$REAL_BATS" "$@"
fi
